VideoToolbox decoding on macOS asks for Metal-compatible pixel buffers instead of
OpenGL-texture-compatible ones. Suggested by @bit24 (Nightjar issue 141,
https://github.com/RamazanKara/nightjar/issues/141), who supplied this one-key change; Moonlight
carries the same change in moonlight-deps (patches/ffmpeg_metal_vt.patch). See also FFmpeg ticket
10789 (https://trac.ffmpeg.org/ticket/10789).

FFmpeg 9.0.2 sets kCVPixelBufferIOSurfaceOpenGLTextureCompatibilityKey in the destination pixel
buffer attributes of every macOS VideoToolbox session, so the decoder produces pictures an OpenGL
texture can wrap, at a cost on every frame. Nightjar's Mac app has no OpenGL path: it presents
through SDL's Metal renderer or reads the planes back into memory. Asking for
kCVPixelBufferMetalCompatibilityKey instead (macOS 10.11 and later) gives IOSurface-backed buffers
that Metal can use directly. iOS keeps its OpenGL ES key; nothing else changes.

This modifies libavcodec/videotoolbox.c, part of FFmpeg, licensed under the GNU Lesser General
Public License 2.1 or later (see the file's own notice and FFmpeg's COPYING.LGPLv2.1, which
macos-deps.sh ships with the libraries); the change is under the same terms. Applies to FFmpeg
9.0.2 as written.

diff --git a/libavcodec/videotoolbox.c b/libavcodec/videotoolbox.c
index 8df8a32..d4d28b5 100644
--- a/libavcodec/videotoolbox.c
+++ b/libavcodec/videotoolbox.c
@@ -822,7 +822,7 @@ static CFDictionaryRef videotoolbox_buffer_attributes_create(int width,
 #if TARGET_OS_IPHONE
     CFDictionarySetValue(buffer_attributes, kCVPixelBufferOpenGLESCompatibilityKey, kCFBooleanTrue);
 #else
-    CFDictionarySetValue(buffer_attributes, kCVPixelBufferIOSurfaceOpenGLTextureCompatibilityKey, kCFBooleanTrue);
+    CFDictionarySetValue(buffer_attributes, kCVPixelBufferMetalCompatibilityKey, kCFBooleanTrue);
 #endif

     CFRelease(io_surface_properties);
